  "answer_core": "I would not grant Marcus 15% equity, a CTO title, and a board seat on Friday. Those are irreversible commitments, and several critical dependencies currently run through him at once: the platform prototype, technical delivery, Jake and Lina, client confidence, and part of the exit story. Granting the full package now prices his contribution and concentrates risk before you know whether he reduces or increases it.\n\nFriday should be a direct, structured conversation about a staged path, not a vague delay or flat rejection. A vague \"later\" reads as avoidance and can trigger exactly what you want to prevent: Marcus disengaging, pulling Jake and Lina with him, slowing the platform, or destabilizing client continuity.\n\nYour position is that there can be a path to major upside and leadership, but only after evidence. Define a short sprint that tests whether he delivers the key platform work, whether that work moves revenue, whether team continuity improves rather than worsens, whether knowledge transfer happens, whether his behavior fits the governance you need, and whether he strengthens the company beyond himself. Spell out what success in that window would unlock.\n\nKeep intermediate structures alive, but do not turn the conversation into a catalog of instruments. Use exit math only as false-precision caution; until buyer assumptions, platform value, and departure scenarios are tested, the numbers should not carry the decision.\n\nGo in ready to say you want to explore a serious path, but you cannot make irreversible governance and ownership commitments before proving what the partnership does to the company.",
